Actually, the fastest Java entry I recall builds the tree as an array of nodes per level:

```java
private static TreeNode[] createTree(int depth) {
    TreeNode[] level = new TreeNode[1 << depth]; // leaves
    // fill with leaf nodes
    for (int i = 0; i < level.length; i++) level[i] = new TreeNode(null, null);
    for (int d = depth - 1; d >= 0; d--) {
        TreeNode[] next = new TreeNode[1 << d];
        for (int i = 0; i < next.length; i++) {
            next[i] = new TreeNode(level[2*i], level[2*i+1]);
        }
        level = next;
    }
    return level;
}
```

This allocates the same number of node objects but in a cache-friendly order (children allocated before parents, contiguous within levels). This improves GC throughput because generations are more compact.

